  <h2>5. Editorial standards</h2>
  <p>
    DaoismHub publishes under an explicit evidence discipline: primary sources, scholarly
    interpretation, practitioner reports and editorial analysis are never conflated, and
    every page carries an evidence class. Claims about research report what studies show
    and what they do not prove. The full classification system is documented in the
    <a href="https://data.daoismhub.com/schemas/evidence-system/">Evidence System</a> and
    <a href="https://data.daoismhub.com/schemas/data-schema/">Data Schema</a> pages of the
    Data Registry. Corrections can be requested at the contact address below.
  </p>

  <h2>6. Health-claim compliance</h2>
  <p>
    DaoismHub makes no diagnosis, treatment, cure or prevention claims of any kind.
    Meditation and contemplative research is discussed as research — with methods, sample
    sizes and limitations stated — never as medical endorsement. Traditional practice
    descriptions are historical and ethnographic, not recommendations to practice.
  </p>

  <h2>7. Practitioner experience records</h2>
  <p>
    The experience collection documents anonymized, paraphrased reports from public
    community discussions. No usernames, locations or identifying details are retained.
    These records are presented as practitioner self-reports only, never as evidence of
    efficacy or safety.
  </p>

